Yomonsni has a basis in whole person development. The practice utilizes many aspects of human psychology with the intent of assisting, motivating and encouraging the development and growth of an individuals mind, body and self.

In regular practice there will be physical intimate touch to all areas of the body for purposes of assessment, relaxation, pleasure; use of persuasion, influence, hypnosis to challenge and develop the mind/self

While the initiate is being charged for the time they spend with the facilitator, what they are paying for is directly for the facilities and consumables used; a basic stipend for the facilitator. The fee is not changed for the nature of the touch/massage, for the grooming services used as bonding, 


At no time will the facilitator touch in a way that is not consenting; make reference to or suggest starting a romantic relationship with the initiate; use untruths or fabrications or deny that which was said or stated.

The influence, persuasion and hypnosis used will be used for the benefit and growth of the initiate, not to a specific end or belief, but to the nature of the initiate themselves.


In general, the Yomonsni Ethical Code is focused on four main areas for the facilitators

   2) Strive for the betterment of participants
   3) Open truth and honesty in all dealings
   1) Informed consent in all dealings
   4) Non-judgemental acceptance


   Informed consent is a fundamental concept, simply by virtue that this shows respect between people. Consent and agreement before action is fairly easily understood, however the concept of informed is one that is even less practiced in the broader world. Informed consent can not be obtained by deception, coersion, ignorance or manipulation. The one consenting must understand what is being consented to and the consequences that may arise from that consent.

   Striving for the betterment of participants is akin to the Hippocratic oath to "Do no harm", but tempered with the understanding that sometimes unknown harms arise directly or indirectly from the actions, words or insturctions given. Sometimes harm may be necessary to truly get to betterment - confronting a past trauma, pushing through discomfort of exercise to build strength, enduring the pain of stretching to become more flexible, confronting untruths in order to gain wisdom.  Though harm (short or long term) may arise, there is never an intent for anything other than the long term betterment of the participants. 

   Open truth and honesty should be a fundamental throughout life, but sadly, this is not the case. Saying that a product 'may assist in weight loss' is irresponsible without showing studies that are at least ambiguous to the effect. Open truth and honesty would see such a promotion need to state that 'no studies have been done but we hope that this works'. This open truth and honesty is the fundamental core by which informed consent can be attained. 

   Non-judgemental acceptance is what it sounds like. You, what you share, who you are, your background and choices in life, are accepted without judgement. Your past is accepted, your future is in flux and will be influenced by your journey in Yomonsni.



As a participant the ethics that are expected of you
   0) Critical thinking tempered with openness to new ideas
   1) Honor your commitments and consent
   2) Full disclosure and total openness of self
   3) Non-judgemental acceptance
   4) Effort


   Critical thinking is important, analyze, question, integrate information given to you, and how it best applies to you. 


   Honoring your commitment and consent is basic. If you consent to something, within the Yomonsni ethics of informed consent, you are expected to hold to it. If you commit to a course of action, whether booking a session, taking an action for your own growth or how you will treat others, do it.

   Much like the Yomonsni code of open truth and honesty, you are expected to fully open yourself and share everything into the Yomonsni facilitator and society. Only by holding nothing back can you expereince complete acceptance and freedom. 

   Non-judgemental acceptance is just that. You listen, accept, do, that which is directed of you by your facilitator. You accept direction, criticism, taking it on board for analysis and application of your critical thinking skills. 


   Effort in this context means that you exert effort in order to participate, to follow the instructions
